## Entry-Level Grading

For each entry (experience or project), produce:

```
Entry: {name}
Hook Grade: {A/B/C/D/F}
Keep: {Yes / Yes but deprioritize / Replace / Cut}
Hook Assessment: {1 sentence from the hiring manager's perspective — what you thought reading the first bullet}
Weakest Bullet: {quote it} → {what's wrong with it}
Strongest Bullet: {quote it} → {why it works}
Role Fit: {how relevant this entry is to THIS specific role — not generically}
```

---

## Grading Output Format

```
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
RESUME GRADING REPORT
Role: {Role} at {Company}
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

REVIEWER PERSONA
{3-5 sentences establishing who you are as the reviewer — the company, the team, the bar you're holding}

DIMENSION SCORES
─────────────────
Hook Quality:          {A/B/C/D} — {1-line rationale}
ATS Coverage:          {A/B/C/D} — {n}/{total} keywords present in bullets
Metric Density:        {A/B/C/D} — {1-line rationale}
Tech Justification:    {A/B/C/D} — {1-line rationale}
Role Relevance:        {A/B/C/D} — {1-line rationale}
Narrative Coherence:   {A/B/C/D} — {1-line rationale}
Skills Line Fit:       {A/B/C/D} — {1-line rationale; note if skills wrap}
Page Fit:              {A/B/C/D} — {1-line rationale; overflow / underfill / balanced}

OVERALL GRADE: {A/B/C/D/F}

ENTRY BREAKDOWN
─────────────────
{Entry-level grading block for each entry — see format above}

WHAT WOULD MAKE ME PASS THIS IMMEDIATELY
─────────────────────────────────────────
{3 specific, concrete things — if these were present, you'd stop reading and move to next step}

WHAT WOULD MAKE ME REJECT THIS IMMEDIATELY
──────────────────────────────────────────
{3 specific red flags — if any of these are present, the resume gets no-piled}

CHANGES REQUIRED BEFORE NEXT PASS
───────────────────────────────────
Priority 1 (blocks passing): {specific edit}
Priority 2 (significantly improves score): {specific edit}
Priority 3 (marginal improvement): {specific edit}
```

Do not edit any files. Your only job is to produce this report.

